      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "Given that the pressure \\(P\\) of a gas is inversely proportional to its volume \\(V\\), we can write this relationship as:\n\n\\[ P \\propto \\frac{1}{V} \\]\n\nThis implies that:\n\n\\[ P_1 \\cdot V_1 = P_2 \\cdot V_2 \\]\n\nwhere:\n- \\(P_1\\) is the initial pressure,\n- \\(V_1\\) is the initial volume,\n- \\(P_2\\) is the final pressure,\n- \\(V_2\\) is the final volume.\n\nWe are given:\n- \\(P_1 = 5 \\text{ kPa}\\),\n- \\(V_1 = 2.28 \\text{ liters}\\),\n- \\(V_2 = 5.7 \\text{ liters}\\).\n\nWe need to find \\(P_2\\).\n\nUsing the relationship \\(P_1 \\cdot V_1 = P_2 \\cdot V_2\\):\n\n\\[ 5 \\text{ kPa} \\cdot 2.28 \\text{ liters} = P_2 \\cdot 5.7 \\text{ liters} \\]\n\nFirst, solve for \\(P_2\\) by isolating it on one side of the equation:\n\n\\[ 11.4 \\text{ kPa} \\cdot \\text{liters} = P_2 \\cdot 5.7 \\text{ liters} \\]\n\nNow, divide both sides by 5.7 liters:\n\n\\[ P_2 = \\frac{11.4 \\text{ kPa} \\cdot \\text{liters}}{5.7 \\text{ liters}} \\]\n\nCalculating the right-hand side:\n\n\\[ P_2 = \\frac{11.4}{5.7} \\text{ kPa} \\approx 2 \\text{ kPa} \\]\n\nTherefore, the new pressure when all oxygen is transferred from the 2.28-liter container to the 5.7-liter container at the same temperature is \\(\\boxed{2}\\) kPa.",
